<title>DENMARK: Danish March North Sea oil production up 4.8 pct.</title>
<headline>Danish March North Sea oil production up 4.8 pct.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1997-04-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Danish Underground Consortium (DUC) oil production from its North Sea fields rose 4.8 percent in March 1997 from February, operator A.P. Moeller Group said on Monday.</p>
<p>It told Reuters that oil production was 961,000 tonnes in March, or 227,742 barrels per day (bpd), compared with 217,246 bpd in February.</p>
<p>DUC is responsible for virtually the entire output from Denmark's North Sea oil and gas fields. Denmark is the third-biggest producer in western Europe after Norway and Britain. Production data (figures in 1,000 bpd):</p>
<p>Field			March 97	   Feb 97	March 96</p>
<p>Dan			   67.8		69.8	   64.5</p>
<p>Kraka			  6.2		 5.6	    7.0</p>
<p>Regnar			 0.0		 0.0	    1.0</p>
<p>Gorm			  49.3		46.8	   49.9</p>
<p>Skjold			34.8		33.1	   37.5</p>
<p>Rolf			   4.3		 3.5	    3.7</p>
<p>Dagmar			 0.3		 0.3	    0.5</p>
<p>Tyra			  26.9		25.9	   29.0</p>
<p>Valdemar		     2.6		 2.2	    2.4</p>
<p>Roar			   8.5		 9.7	    5.4</p>
<p>Svend			 27.0		20.2	    --</p>
<p>Harald			 0.0		 --	     --</p>
<p>Total			227.7	     217.2	   200.7</p>
<p>A.P. Moeller Group owns 39 percent of DUC. Its two partners, Royal Dutch/Shell Group and Texaco Inc own 46 percent and 15 percent respectively.</p>
<p>-- Chris Follett, Copenhagen newsroom, +45-33-969652</p>
